      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>What differs</th>
          <th>Lateral entry MBA (as actually offered)</th>
          <th>Regular 2-year MBA</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {comparisonRows.map((item) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="What differs"><strong>{item.item}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Lateral entry MBA (as actually offered)">{item.lateral}</td>
            <td data-label="Regular 2-year MBA">{item.regular}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Details vary by institute and change from year to year. Always confirm the current rule directly with
    the specific college's admission office and cross-check it against the regulator's own published list,
    not a third-party summary, including this one.
  </p>

  <h2 id="colleges">Where this route genuinely exists</h2>

  <p>
    Lateral entry MBA is not offered by every business school, and it is not a route pushed by IIMs or
    most top-ranked institutes, which run standard 2-year programs admitted through CAT, XAT, or GMAT.
    Where it does exist, it tends to show up at private and state universities running PGDM or MBA
    programs with their own admission flexibility, sometimes marketed through education-services platforms
    that help students apply across multiple partner campuses, and occasionally at individual private
    universities offering it as a defined, named bridge program with a set fee and duration.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      Because this route is uncommon among the highest-ranked institutes, the presence of a "lateral entry
      MBA" option is not, by itself, a signal of program quality. Judge the specific institute on its own
      placement record, faculty, and recognition status, the same way you would judge a regular MBA there.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="red-flags">Recognition risk and red flags to check before you pay</h2>

  <p>
    This is the part of the decision that carries the most real financial and career risk, and it is
    where a lateral-entry MBA search differs most sharply from a lateral-entry BTech search. Engineering
    lateral entry is governed uniformly by AICTE, so the recognition question rarely comes up. MBA lateral
    entry has no equivalent single authority guaranteeing every program labelled this way, which means the
    burden of verification falls on you.
  </p>

  <p>
    India's University Grants Commission has previously clarified, in response to a Right to Information
    query, that an MBA is treated as a 2-year course under its guidelines, and regulators have separately
    warned students against fake advertisements for ultra-compressed "1-year" or even "10-day" MBA
    programs. The UGC has also identified more than 20 fake or unrecognised universities operating in
    India, some of which have promoted "one sitting" or accelerated degree options that carry no real
    legal standing for jobs, government eligibility, or further study.
  </p>

  <p>
    None of that means every lateral-entry MBA is fake. It means the label alone tells you nothing about
    legitimacy, and you have to check three things yourself before paying any fee:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Is the university itself currently listed as UGC-recognised, with an active, verifiable status for the current academic year?</li>
    <li>Is the specific MBA/PGDM program AICTE-approved (where AICTE approval applies), not just the university in general?</li>
    <li>Does the final degree certificate read identically to the institute's standard 2-year MBA, with no separate lateral-entry notation that could raise questions later with an employer, a government recruiter, or a doctoral admissions committee?</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="alternatives">Regular MBA and Executive MBA: the two steadier alternatives</h2>

  <p>
    Before committing to a lateral-entry route, it is worth comparing it honestly against the two paths
    most working professionals and graduates actually use to reach the same goal.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Regular 2-year MBA</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Best for fresh graduates or professionals with 0-3 years of experience.</li>
        <li>Admission through CAT, XAT, GMAT, CMAT, or MAT; work experience helps but is not mandatory at most institutes.</li>
        <li>Widest institute choice, including every IIM and most top-ranked B-schools.</li>
        <li>Lowest recognition risk when the institute itself is properly accredited.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Executive MBA</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Built specifically for experienced professionals, typically requiring 2-5 years of work experience, with some premium programs asking for 10+ years.</li>
        <li>Usually accepts GMAT or GRE, with some institutes also accepting CAT, XAT, or an internal exam.</li>
        <li>Designed around working schedules, often weekend or hybrid formats, without pretending to compress a full syllabus into a shorter timeline the way some lateral-entry marketing does.</li>
        <li>Generally carries clearer, more consistent recognition standards across reputable institutes than an ad-hoc lateral-entry program.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    If your only real qualification for a lateral-entry MBA is several years of work experience, an
    Executive MBA at a properly accredited institute is usually the safer, more respected route to the
    same outcome: faster entry that acknowledges what you already know, without the recognition ambiguity
    that a smaller, less-standardised lateral-entry label can carry.
  </p>

  <p>
    Treat the fee for any lateral-entry, regular, or Executive MBA as a strict planning check, not just a
    number on a brochure: a common heuristic is to keep total course fees within roughly 10% of your total
    education budget unless you can point to specific, program-level evidence that justifies going higher —
    things like verified placement outcomes, genuine faculty and industry access, or a licensing/accreditation
    requirement, not the institute's brand name alone. Spending materially above that on an MBA whose main
    selling point is "you finish faster" rarely builds the wider, high-income skill portfolio — real subject
    depth, applied projects, network, and communication — that actually moves your income and career options
    forward. A degree only earns a premium price when it demonstrably builds that portfolio, not when it
    only saves a year.
  </p>

  <h2 id="checkpoint">Use The 4-Checkpoint Protocol before you apply</h2>

  <p>
    Because this route is institute-specific rather than nationally standardised, the usual "just check
    the exam pattern" advice does not apply cleanly. Run these four checkpoints instead.
  </p>

  <div class="protocol-stack">
